FEATURES OF EXT2:
Ext2 stands for second extended file system
Ext2 does not have journaling features 
On flash drives, sub drives, ext2 is recommended 
Maximum individual file size can be from 16 GB to 2 TB
Over all ext2 file system size can be form 2 TB to 32 TB
FEATURES OF EXT3:
Ext3 stands for third extended file system
The main benefit of ext3 is that allows journaling
Journaling has a dedicated area in the file system where all the challenges are tracked when the file system crashes the possibility of file system corruption is less because of journaling 
Maximum individual file size can be from 16GB to 2TB
Over all ext3 file system size can be from 2 TB to 32 TB
You can convert a ext2 file system to ext3 file system directly (without backup / restore)
FEATURES OF EXT4:
Ext4 stands for fourth extended file system
It was introduced in 2008
Starting from Linux kernel 2.6.19 ext4 was available 
Maximum individual file size can be from 16 GB to 16 TB
Overall maximum ext4 file system size is 1 EB (Exabytes) 
Directory can contain a maximum of 64000 subdirectories (as opposed to 32000 in ext3)
