 RAID(REDUNDANT ARRAY OF INDEPENDENT DISKS OR EXPENSIVE DISKS):  Raid is a storage technology that combines multiple disks drive components into a logical unit. Data is distributed across the drives in one of several ways called raid levels depending on what level of redundancy and performance (via parallel communication)is required.
Fault tolerance:
Raid 0 = striping: The disks are 2 with equal size max 32 disks. it writes the data parallel on multiple disks and Data writing is fast.
Disadvantages:
1) If one disk fails, the raid volume will fail.
2) No data redundancy.
3) No fault tolerance.
Raid 1= mirroring: the disks 2 with equal size. it writes the same data on  multiple disks. If one disk fails we can access the data from another disk.
Advantages: 1) It has a redundancy 2) It has a fault tolerance
Disadvantages: 1) Cost effective 2) Data write operations are slow.
 Raid 5 = striping with parity: the disks are 3 with equal size maximum disks are 32. it writes the data parallel on multiple disks with a parity information of other 2 disks. a parity is calculated to reconstruct the data when a disk fails in the volume.
