If your application server in AWS is using 90%+ CPU and is unreachable (e.g., SSH not working or app down), follow these real-time troubleshooting steps to identify and fix the issue.
High CPU usage (90%+), Server is not accessible
Possible causes: infinite loops, memory leak, traffic spike, etc.
🔍 Step 1: Check CloudWatch Metrics
Go to AWS CloudWatch → EC2 → Metrics → CPUUtilization
Identify when the CPU spiked and how long it’s sustained.
🧠 Why: Confirms if it’s a temporary spike or ongoing issue.
🧪 Step 2: Use EC2 Instance Recovery Techniques
✅ Option A: Use EC2 Systems Manager (SSM) to run commands
Works only if SSM agent is installed & IAM role is attached
Go to AWS Systems Manager → Session Manager
Start a remote shell session
Run: top, htop, ps -aux --sort=-%cpu
Kill the high CPU process using: kill -9 <PID>
✅ Option B: Stop & Start the Instance
Last resort – IP will change unless EIP is attached
Go to EC2 console → Actions → Stop Instance
Then Start it again.
Check app logs once restarted.
🧠 Be aware that this resets the CPU state but doesn’t fix the root cause.
✅ Option C: Increase Instance Size (Vertical Scaling)
Select instance → Actions → Instance Settings → Change Instance Type
Upgrade to larger instance (e.g., t3.medium → t3.large)
Start the instance again.
🧠 Useful if CPU bottleneck is due to load and not app bug.
🛠️ Step 3: Enable Auto Scaling (Prevention for Next Time)
Create an Auto Scaling Group (ASG) with:
CPU threshold policy (e.g., launch new instance at >70% CPU)
Attach Load Balancer (ALB) to route traffic across instances.
🧰 Step 4: Fix App-Level Root Cause
After the server is accessible:
Check logs: /var/log/messages, /var/log/syslog, or app logs.
Check for:
Infinite loops
Bad cron jobs
Heavy queries
Memory leaks
