You're running 3 applications (App1, App2, App3) on one EC2 instance, and want end users to access each application separately — for example, go directly to Application 3.
There are multiple ways to route traffic to each application, depending on how the apps are deployed (ports, paths, domains, etc.).
✅ Option 1: Use Different Ports (Quick and Simple)
Each application runs on a different port:
App
URL Format (public IP or domain)
App1
http://<EC2-IP>:8080
App2
http://<EC2-IP>:8081
App3
http://<EC2-IP>:8082

➡ ️ Expose the EC2's security group to allow incoming traffic on these ports.
✅ Option 2: Use Nginx or Apache Reverse Proxy (Production Ready)
Run Nginx or Apache as a reverse proxy to route traffic like:
URL
Routes to
http://<EC2-IP>/app1
App1
http://<EC2-IP>/app2
App2
http://<EC2-IP>/app3
App3

Example Nginx config:
server {
  listen 80;

  location /app1/ {
    proxy_pass http://localhost:8080/;
  }

  location /app2/ {
    proxy_pass http://localhost:8081/;
  }

  location /app3/ {
    proxy_pass http://localhost:8082/;
  }
}
📌 Cleaner URLs, one public IP, no need for extra ports.
✅ Option 3: Use Subdomains with Reverse Proxy + Route 53 (Advanced)
If you have a domain, use subdomains:
Subdomain
App
app1.example.com
App1
app2.example.com
App2
app3.example.com
App3

Configure Nginx to reverse proxy each subdomain to the correct app.
Use Route 53 or any DNS provider to map subdomains to EC2’s public IP.
📌 Best for production; allows SSL for each subdomain.
✅ Option 4: Use an Application Load Balancer (ALB)
ALB supports path-based or host-based routing:
app1.domain.com → Target group 1 (App1)
app2.domain.com → Target group 2 (App2)
app3.domain.com → Target group 3 (App3)
Even though all apps are on one EC2, you can register the same instance multiple times with different ports in each target group.
📌 Enterprise-grade solution with auto scaling support.
🔐 Don’t Forget:
Security Groups must allow inbound traffic on the ports used by the apps or proxy.
Set up SSL (HTTPS) using Let’s Encrypt if public access is needed.
For domain names, use Route 53 or your DNS provider.
